-- title:
--   Normality of completed local rings at supersingular points
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$, and a nonzero $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$. Let $A_0$ be a discrete valuation domain with finite residue field whose maximal ideal is generated by $q$, in which $\ell_g$ and $M'$ are units. Assume the three equivariance hypotheses needed to build the level data: $\Gamma_1$-points (a point on the affine curve with $\mathrm{pre}\Psi_{\ell_g}$ vanishing at its abscissa, with the two marked points equal) are preserved by variable change; cyclic-generator kernel polynomials of prime-power level are preserved under `kernelVariableChangeDeg`; and divisibility of `inLineMulPoly` is likewise preserved. Let $\mathcal G$ be a family of relative group laws on projective Weierstrass models over $A_0$-algebras with unit discriminant, chord-and-tangent and with identity at the origin chart, and $\mathcal T$ a level transport of Drinfeld pairs compatible with sections; assume graded homomorphisms realising variable change and coefficient change on the projective model rings exist, with the stated bound on irrelevant ideals. Let $P_0$ be a fine moduli package for the datum `rigidDataH1Pow`, with $B_0$ of finite type over $A_0$, and $x$ a raw datum over $B_0$ representing the universal point. Let $\mathfrak m \subset B_0$ be maximal containing $q$, and $F_0$ a formal group over $B_0/\mathfrak m$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of the reduction of $x$'s curve, with $F_0$'s $q$-th series equal to a unit times $X^{q^2}$. Then the $\mathfrak m$-adic completion of $B_0$ is a domain, is integrally closed, and the image of $q$ in it is nonzero.
--
--   This is the supersingular case of the local structure of the fine moduli ring of the $H_1$ moduli problem: at a closed point of residue characteristic $q$ where the formal group of the reduced universal curve has height two, the completed local ring is a two-dimensional regular, hence normal, local ring in which $q$ does not vanish. It feeds the statements on flatness and normality of the quotient by a minimal prime and on the identification of the completion modulo $q$ used in the unramifiedness argument.
